    "model": "whisper-1 via Hermes managed openai-audio gateway",
    "expected": "On the largest scales, cosmology expects the universe to look the same in every direction. A conceptual sky of spiral galaxies should therefore show no built-in preference for clockwise or anticlockwise winding. Galaxies acquire angular momentum while surrounding structure grows, so balance is the prediction, not an accident. A preference for spiral galaxies in one sector of the sky to be left-handed or right-handed spirals would indicate a parity violating asymmetry in the overall universe and a preferred axis. Longo reported: An unbinned analysis for a dipole component that made no prior assumptions for the dipole axis gives a dipole asymmetry of \u22120.0408\u00b10.011 with a probability of occurring by chance of 7.9\u00d710\u207b\u2074. Shamir reported: The results show that the local universe (z<0.3) is not isotropic in terms of galaxy spin, with probability P<5.8\u00d710\u207b\u2076 of such asymmetry to occur by chance. Land and colleagues reported: After establishing and correcting for a certain level of bias in our handedness results we find the winding sense of the galaxies to be consistent with statistical isotropy. These studies reported incompatible answers, so the literature remains disputed; this video adopts none of them as its finding. The catch is that people and algorithms can carry a left-right sorting bias, so the same images need a mirror control.",
    "transcript": "On the largest scales, cosmology expects the universe to look the same in every direction. A conceptual sky of spiral galaxies should therefore show no built-in preference for clockwise or anticlockwise winding. Galaxies acquire angular momentum while surrounding structure grows, so balance is the prediction, not an accident. A preference for spiral galaxies in one sector of the sky to be left-handed or right-handed spirals would indicate a parity-violating asymmetry in the overall universe and a preferred axis. Longo reported, An unbinned analysis for a dipole component that made no prior assumptions for the dipole axis gives a dipole asymmetry of minus 0.0408 plus or minus 0.011, with a probability of occurring by chance of 7.9 times 10 to the minus fourth. Shamir reported, The results show that the local universe, z less than 0.3, is not isotropic in terms of galaxy spin, with probability p less than 5.8 times 10 to the minus sixth of such asymmetry to occur by chance. Land and colleagues reported, After establishing and correcting for a certain level of bias in our handedness results, we find the winding sense of the galaxies to be consistent with statistical isotropy. These studies reported incompatible answers, so the literature remains disputed. This video adopts none of them as its finding. The catch is that people and algorithms can carry a left-right sorting bias, so the same images need a mirror control.",
